## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## branch_improve_loop  (v2 — one agent reviews+improves the branch
##                       diff in its natural flow, minimal framing)
##
## THE MISSION is a REVIEW campaign scoped to the BRANCH DIFF: review and
## improve the changes THIS branch introduces over `base_ref` (default
## "main") — the diff `git diff $(git merge-base <base_ref> HEAD)` measured
## against the WORKING TREE (so a prior pass's uncommitted fixes stay
## visible; the commit-only `base_ref...HEAD` form would hide them and the
## loop could never converge). It finds the REAL issues the change introduces
## or leaves — bugs, regressions, missing/weak tests, unhandled errors,
## quality problems IN THE DIFF — and improves them, committing each fix in
## stride. It does NOT re-litigate code the branch didn't touch.
##
## Sibling of `whole_improve_loop` v2: SAME shape (one adaptive `campaign`
## agent + a deterministic build/test gate + a bounded continuation loop +
## git-as-state + the opt-in MR path), DIFFERENT scope — whole_improve_loop
## applies ONE determined axis across the whole codebase; branch_improve_loop
## reviews+improves the diff of ONE branch. `whole_improve_loop`'s AXIS is
## replaced here by the branch diff itself: `base_ref` + the diff define the
## work.
##
## WHY THIS SHAPE. The v1 branch loop (ADR-011 chunking → ADR-052 mono/dual)
## was ~11 nodes of chunked cross-family review machinery
## (plan_chunks → alt → reviewer_claude/reviewer_gpt → streak_check →
## fix_claude/fix_gpt → prepare_commit → commit_changes). It OVER-FRAMED the
## work — the same lesson whole_improve_loop learned (ADR-058,
## docs/references/productive-session-patterns.md): "the deficit is framing,
## not capability"; "once framed, a campaign runs itself". A capable agent
## reviews a branch diff and fixes what it finds better as ONE flow — a living
## todo list, one verified commit per fix, in stride — than as an assembly
## line of chunk/review/fix/commit nodes.
##
## GRAPH (see the workflow block):
##   campaign     — ONE adaptive agent (claude_code, full tools). Mission =
##                  review + improve the branch diff, with a standing-autonomy
##                  contract that reproduces the productive-session shape
##                  AGENT-SIDE: read the branch diff, build a LIVING todo of
##                  the concrete real issues the change introduces or leaves,
##                  then the repeated unit locate → smallest fix → build →
##                  test → semantic COMMIT, one issue at a time, committing
##                  each fix AS it finishes. git IS the durable state — a
##                  re-dispatch re-runs `campaign`, which reads `git log` +
##                  re-diffs the branch and continues. No worklist/cursor
##                  scratch file. Termination output: {branch_clean,
##                  commits_this_pass, issues_remaining, needs_human, …}.
##   verify_build — adaptive, stack-agnostic: reads the `verify-build` skill
##     + verify_run  and writes the repo's real build+test into an OUT-OF-TREE
##                  <scratch_dir>/verify.sh; a DETERMINISTIC tool node
##                  re-runs it and gates on the REAL exit code (no LLM
##                  judgment). The tight real-feedback loop AND the
##                  anti-Goodhart truth oracle: the agent can't self-certify.
##   gate         — deterministic compute: green AND branch_clean → converged
##                  → ship; else loop back to `campaign` (RED → with the
##                  failure log so the agent fixes what it broke; green but
##                  issues remain → another pass). No LLM, no shell.
##   mr_gate      — deterministic: open the optional MR/PR (the series of
##     + finalize_mr per-pass commits) when open_mr, else finish.
##
## CONTINUATION IS BOUNDED. The single declared loop
## (campaign→verify_build→verify_run→gate→campaign) is capped by
## `max_passes`; on exhaustion it ships what is banked. `iterion validate`
## reports NO undeclared cycle. LANDS CONTINUOUSLY: the agent commits each
## fix in stride, so an interrupted / budget-capped run has banked every
## committed fix (git is the state).
##
## RIGHT-ARTIFACT (docs/workflow_authoring_pitfalls.md): the campaign runs
## `git add -N .` BEFORE diffing so a branch that ADDS files shows those files
## in `git diff` (untracked files are otherwise invisible to `git diff`), and
## commits the UNCOMMITTED working tree AFTER its own build+test passes,
## staging untracked files (`git add -A`) so a fix that adds files actually
## lands; the deterministic verify gate then re-checks the committed tree.
##
## STACK/REPO-AGNOSTIC (CLAUDE.md "Catalog bots are repo-agnostic" +
## "Universal code bots"): the base_ref + the diff define the work. No
## language / package-manager literal, and no iterion-specific target path,
## appears in any var default, command body, or schema — campaign /
## verify_build are adaptive agents that read whatever repo they are pointed
## at.
##
## RETIRED FROM v1 (git history has the design): plan_chunks (+ the chunking
## dials), the `alt` condition router, reviewer_claude / reviewer_gpt, the
## streak_check convergence compute, fix_claude / fix_gpt, prepare_commit /
## commit_changes, and the review_mode / mono_family topology vars. The
## campaign self-reviews via a fresh re-review of the diff + the deterministic
## build/test gate + its own judgment (the operator's proven manual pattern),
## and commits each fix in stride — so the ADR-052 mono/dual review topology no
## longer applies to this bot (no reviewer nodes); it stays on the other
## review-loop bots. 11 nodes → 6.
## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

vars:
  workspace_dir: string = "${PROJECT_DIR}"

  ## Branch comparison base. The campaign reviews the diff
  ## `git diff $(git merge-base {{vars.base_ref}} HEAD)` — the changes
  ## introduced by the current branch on top of `base_ref`, measured against
  ## the WORKING TREE so a prior pass's uncommitted fixes are included (the
  ## commit-only `...HEAD` form would hide them and break convergence).
  ## Default "main" covers the GitHub/GitLab/Forgejo conventions; pass
  ## `--var base_ref=develop` (or any local ref) when reviewing against a
  ## different integration branch.
  base_ref: string = "main"

  ## Free-form extra context for the campaign agent (e.g. what the branch is
  ## supposed to do, known caveats). Empty = the diff speaks for itself.
  scope_notes: string = ""

  ## Out-of-tree, sandbox-writable scratch for the deterministic gate's
  ## verify script/log ONLY (no chunk files / worklist any more — git is the
  ## state). The engine resolves ${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR} to
  ## ~/.iterion/projects/<key>/scratch non-sandboxed and to the
  ## container-writable /tmp/iterion-scratch under a sandbox (never hardcode
  ## ~/.iterion here — the -full image's pinned non-host User cannot write a
  ## host-owned path → EACCES). Keeping it OUT of the target worktree is the
  ## repo-agnostic rule: never pollute someone else's tree.
  scratch_dir: string = "${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR}/branch-improve-loop"

  ## BASELINE (G5 — docs/references/productive-session-patterns.md). Known
  ## pre-existing test failures / flaky tests the campaign must SKIP rather
  ## than burn budget proving they aren't the branch's own. Empty (default) =
  ## the campaign establishes it once cheaply (a `git stash`-based check
  ## against base_ref) then skips what predates the branch. The
  ## dispatcher/operator can pre-fill it.
  baseline: string = ""

  ## Hard cap on continuation PASSES — the convergence backstop. Each pass is
  ## one campaign→verify cycle; the run stops (shipping what is banked) when
  ## the branch is reported clean + the tree is green, or when this cap is
  ## hit. Sizes the declared continuation loop. Raise it (with the budget) for
  ## a large branch diff; the agent commits in stride, so every pass banks
  ## real, landed work.
  max_passes: int = 8

  ## ─── MR/PR finalization (opt-in) ───────────────────────────────
  ## When open_mr=true, after convergence finalize_mr pushes the run's HEAD
  ## (the SERIES of per-pass commits) to the forge and opens a merge/pull
  ## request. Off by default so a plain local run just leaves the commits on
  ## the operator's branch. The comment→MR trigger sets this true. This bot
  ## runs in a per-run git worktree (worktree: auto), so the commits advance
  ## the worktree HEAD and finalize_mr pushes that branch. Requires a
  ## forge_token secret (or host-authenticated gh/glab).
  open_mr: bool = false
  ## Branch name to push + open the MR from. Empty = derive a stable name
  ## from the run (iterion/improve/<run-id>).
  mr_branch: string = ""
  ## Integration base the MR targets. Empty = vars.base_ref.
  mr_base: string = ""
  ## Optional back-link target: the source issue this run answers, as
  ## "native:<id>" (local board) or a forge issue URL. When set, finalize_mr
  ## posts the opened MR's URL back as a comment on it.
  source_issue_ref: string = ""

secrets:
  ## Forge access for push + MR creation + the issue back-link. Mounted as a
  ## read-only file the gh/glab CLI authenticates from; never read into a
  ## prompt. optional:true so manual/local runs relying on host-authenticated
  ## gh/glab still work. See skills/forge-mr-create.md.
  forge_token:
    as: file
    optional: true

## ─── Prompts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

prompt campaign_system:
  You are a senior engineer running a focused REVIEW-AND-IMPROVE campaign on
  ONE branch's diff — working EXACTLY as you would in a productive,
  human-driven engineering session. You have the repo in front of you and full
  tools (read, edit, shell, git). No one micro-manages you; you own this pass
  end to end.

  THE SCOPE is the BRANCH DIFF, not the whole codebase. You are reviewing and
  improving the changes THIS branch introduces over `base_ref` (in your user
  prompt). FIRST read the diff — run `git add -N .` (intent-to-add, so any NEW
  files the branch adds show up) then
  `git diff $(git merge-base <base_ref> HEAD)` (merge-base vs WORKING TREE, so
  your OWN uncommitted fixes stay visible; the commit-only `<base_ref>...HEAD`
  form would hide them and the loop could never converge). Prepend
  `-c safe.directory='*'` if git reports "dubious ownership". Enumerate the
  branch footprint with
  `git diff $(git merge-base <base_ref> HEAD) --name-only`. THAT diff is your
  scope — do NOT re-litigate code the branch did not touch (you MAY read the
  surrounding code, callers, conventions ONLY to judge how the branch changes
  integrate).

  HOW TO WORK — reproduce the shape of a productive session, agent-side:

  1. LIVING TODO LIST, not frozen phases. Read the branch diff and build a
     LIVING todo of the CONCRETE, REAL issues the change introduces or leaves
     — bugs, regressions, missing or weak tests, unhandled errors, race
     conditions, security holes, quality problems IN THE DIFF. Keep it DENSE
     and MOVING — re-prioritize, append, and defer items as you learn. Do NOT
     write 5–6 coarse upfront phases and tick them: the list is a metronome
     born from reading the diff, not a plan frozen before it. Cover the
     production-ready axes AS THEY APPLY TO THE DIFF: security (auth, input
     validation, injection, path traversal, secret leaks), robustness (error
     handling, panics, leaked goroutines, missing timeouts, retries without
     backoff), data (migrations, transactions, idempotence, data loss),
     concurrency (unbalanced mutexes, double-close, atomicity), observability,
     performance (O(n^2) on hot paths, N+1), boundaries (recovery, corrupted
     input). A real blocker is a bug/regression/vulnerability/obvious dead
     code — NOT a style preference, naming, or a missing doc.

  2. THE REPEATED UNIT, one issue at a time: locate the exact site in the diff
     → make the SMALLEST fix that resolves the issue there → build → run the
     tests → COMMIT it with a semantic message (`fix(scope): <precise issue>`,
     or the `type(scope):` that fits — `test(...)` for an added test,
     `refactor(...)` for a quality fix, etc.). A few edits per commit,
     validation BEFORE the commit — never commit code you have not built and
     tested green.

  3. COMMIT EACH FIX AS YOU FINISH IT — never batch. Stage everything
     including new files (`git add -A`, so a fix that ADDS a test/helper
     actually lands) and commit before moving on. git IS your durable state:
     an interrupted run keeps every fix you committed, and a fresh pass just
     reads `git log`, re-diffs the branch, and continues where you left off.
     Do NOT keep a separate worklist / cursor file — the todo list lives in
     your working memory, the commits are the record.

  4. BASELINE — don't chase failures the branch didn't cause. Pre-existing
     test failures / known-flaky tests are in your user prompt (`baseline`).
     Do NOT try to fix or investigate those; if a test was already red on
     `base_ref` before this branch, SKIP it and note it. If the baseline is
     empty, do ONE cheap `git stash`-based check (or a run against base_ref)
     to establish which failures pre-date the branch, then stop investigating
     them. Don't burn budget proving a failure isn't the branch's.

  5. ASK ONLY ON A REAL DECISION. You MAY pause for the operator (the
     ask-user tool) on a genuine decision you cannot make on your own — is a
     behaviour intentional, a destructive trade-off, a backward-compat policy
     call. Keep it RARE: most of the time you decide and keep moving.

  KEEP GOING, one issue at a time, until this pass has resolved every real
  issue you can find in the branch diff within your budget. Then STOP and
  report.

  TERMINATION CONTRACT (required structured output — this is how the engine
  detects completion; an under-specified end just gets re-poked):
    - branch_clean: true ONLY when a fresh RE-REVIEW of the branch diff (re-run
      `git diff $(git merge-base <base_ref> HEAD)`) finds NO remaining real
      issue. false if any real issue remains (a later pass continues from your
      commits).
    - commits_this_pass: how many commits you landed this pass.
    - issues_remaining: a concise note of what real issues still need work
      (empty when branch_clean).
    - needs_human / human_note: set if you paused or need an operator decision.
    - summary: what you reviewed and what you improved, and how it relates to
      the branch's changes.

  Be honest. A DETERMINISTIC build/test gate re-checks the tree after you and
  the run loops you back for another pass until you report branch_clean AND the
  tree is green — so under-reporting only costs a pass, and over-reporting
  lands you right back here.

prompt campaign_user:
  Repository: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  BRANCH BASE (review the diff THIS branch introduces on top of it):
  {{vars.base_ref}}

  Read the diff first: `git add -N .` then
  `git diff $(git merge-base {{vars.base_ref}} HEAD)` (merge-base vs WORKING
  TREE — a prior pass's uncommitted fixes stay in scope; commit-only
  `{{vars.base_ref}}...HEAD` would hide them). Enumerate the footprint with
  `git diff $(git merge-base {{vars.base_ref}} HEAD) --name-only`. That diff
  is your scope — do NOT flag pre-existing code the branch didn't touch.

  Extra context / notes (empty = the diff speaks for itself):
  {{vars.scope_notes}}

  BASELINE — pre-existing failures / known-flaky tests to SKIP (empty =
  establish it once cheaply against {{vars.base_ref}}, then skip what predates
  the branch):
  {{vars.baseline}}

  Deterministic gate feedback from the PREVIOUS pass (empty on the first pass;
  when present, the build/test tree was RED after your last pass — FIX exactly
  what this shows before continuing to review the diff):
  {{input.fail_log}}

  Review the branch diff and improve it one issue at a time — locate →
  smallest fix → build → test → commit — until a fresh re-review finds no real
  issue remaining. Read `git log` first to see what earlier passes already
  fixed. Then report the termination contract (branch_clean, commits_this_pass,
  issues_remaining).

## ─── verify (deterministic build/test gate, stack-agnostic) ─────────
## An ADAPTIVE agent (verify_build) reads the verify-build skill and writes
## the repo's real build+test into <scratch_dir>/verify.sh; a DETERMINISTIC
## tool node (verify_run) re-runs it and gates on the REAL exit code. No
## language / package manager is named here — the gate stays universal while
## the verdict stays deterministic. verify_build does NOT fix code: a RED gate
## routes back to `campaign` (the one capable agent) to fix what it broke.

prompt verify_system:
  You are a release engineer. A campaign pass just improved changes across
  this repository and committed them. Your single job: capture how THIS repo
  builds and tests itself into a re-runnable script the deterministic gate
  will execute. You do NOT change code.

  STEPS:
  1. Detect how THIS repo builds and tests itself — read the `verify-build`
     skill. Use the repo's OWN toolchain (a task runner / Makefile target, a
     package-manager test script, or a language build+test command),
     HONOURING any pinned toolchain (version managers, lockfiles, devbox,
     GOTOOLCHAIN). Do NOT assume a language.
  2. Write an executable POSIX-sh script to `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh`
     (an OUT-OF-TREE scratch path, NOT inside the repo — create it first with
     `mkdir -p {{vars.scratch_dir}}`) that runs the repo's build AND its tests
     (or the closest fast equivalent) and exits non-zero on any failure. Keep
     it to the commands a developer of THIS repo would run; no network-heavy
     or destructive steps.
  3. PRE-EXISTING failures: some repos are already red on the base tree,
     before this run touched anything. Scope `verify.sh` to the build plus the
     test targets the campaign's changes actually affect (package/module
     level), so the gate measures THIS run's work and a red base can't absorb
     it — and list any excluded pre-existing failures in your summary.
  4. Run it ONCE to confirm the script itself is valid (the commands resolve).
     You do NOT fix code here: if the build is RED because the campaign broke
     something, the deterministic gate reports it and the campaign fixes it on
     the next pass. Just make `verify.sh` faithfully run the repo's real
     build+test.
  5. If the repo genuinely has no build/test system (pure docs/config), write
     a script that echoes that and exits 0, and say so.

  A deterministic tool node RE-RUNS your script and gates the run on its real
  exit code. Output prepared=true once `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh` exists
  and reflects the repo's real build+test; summary = the command you settled
  on and any pre-existing failures you scoped out.

prompt verify_user:
  Repository to verify: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  Capture the repo's real build + test into `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh`
  (an out-of-tree scratch path — see the system prompt + the `verify-build`
  skill). Do NOT change code; the deterministic gate runs your script and the
  campaign fixes any red on the next pass.

## ─── MR/PR finalization prompts (opt-in) ────────────────────────────

prompt finalize_mr_system:
  You finalize a converged branch-improvement run by opening ONE merge/pull
  request for the SERIES of per-pass commits this run produced, then
  (optionally) posting the MR link back to the source issue. You make NO
  further code changes.

  Read the `forge-mr-create` skill FIRST — it tells you how to detect the
  forge, authenticate from the mounted forge_token, push the branch, and open
  the MR/PR (GitHub via gh, GitLab via glab, Forgejo/Gitea via REST).

  Procedure:
    1. Confirm there are commits to ship: the run's HEAD must be ahead of the
       base (`git rev-list --count <base>..HEAD` > 0). If not, set
       opened=false with skipped_reason "no commits ahead of base" — do NOT
       open an empty MR.
    2. Determine the branch name (mr_branch if set, else a stable
       iterion/improve/<run-id> name) and the base (mr_base if set, else
       base_ref).
    3. Authenticate the matching forge CLI from the mounted forge_token file
       when present; otherwise assume host auth. If neither is available, set
       opened=false with a precise skipped_reason — never pretend.
    4. Push HEAD to the branch on the origin remote, then open the MR/PR from
       that branch into the base, with a clear title + body summarising the
       branch improvements landed (use scope_notes for context).
    5. If source_issue_ref is set, post the opened MR URL back to it: a forge
       issue URL → comment via the same forge CLI; a "native:<id>" ref → use
       the board comment tool. Set back_linked accordingly.

  Return opened, url, branch, back_linked, skipped_reason and a short summary.
  Be honest: report exactly what happened.

prompt finalize_mr_user:
  Workspace: {{input.workspace_dir}}
  Desired branch (may be empty → derive): {{input.mr_branch}}
  Base (may be empty → base_ref): {{input.mr_base}}
  Source issue to back-link (may be empty): {{input.source_issue_ref}}
  Context / scope: {{input.scope_notes}}

  Push the branch, open the MR/PR, post the back-link when a source issue is
  given, and return the structured result.

## ─── Schemas ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## campaign's input: the deterministic gate's failure log from the PREVIOUS
## pass (empty on the first pass and on a green-but-more-work continuation).
schema campaign_input:
  fail_log: string

## campaign's TERMINATION CONTRACT (G2): a machine-checkable output the engine
## keys convergence on. branch_clean is the done-oracle (a fresh re-review of
## the branch diff finds no remaining real issue); the rest is observability.
schema campaign_output:
  branch_clean: bool
  commits_this_pass: int
  issues_remaining: string
  needs_human: bool
  human_note: string
  summary: string

## verify_plan is verify_build's contract: it has WRITTEN <scratch_dir>/verify.sh.
schema verify_plan:
  prepared: bool
  summary: string

## verify_result is the DETERMINISTIC gate's contract. `passed` is set from
## the real process exit code of re-running <scratch_dir>/verify.sh — NOT an
## LLM judgment. `skipped` is true only when no verify script was produced
## (treated as pass but surfaced loudly, never a silent façade).
schema verify_result:
  passed: bool
  skipped: bool
  exit_code: int
  log_tail: string

## gate is the deterministic continuation decision. converged = the tree is
## green AND the campaign reported the branch diff clean. fail_log relays the
## real build failure to the campaign on a RED pass (empty when green).
schema gate_state:
  converged: bool
  fail_log: string

schema mr_gate_output:
  open_mr: bool

schema finalize_mr_input:
  workspace_dir: string
  mr_branch: string
  mr_base: string
  source_issue_ref: string
  scope_notes: string

schema finalize_mr_output:
  opened: bool
  url: string
  branch: string
  back_linked: bool
  skipped_reason: string
  summary: string

## ─── Nodes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## campaign — the one adaptive agent. claude_code with the full native
## toolset (read/edit/shell/git) so it works exactly like a native Claude
## Code session: read the branch diff, a living todo list of real issues, one
## verified commit per fix, in stride. session: fresh — a fresh pass
## re-derives its todo list from the branch diff + `git log` (git is the
## durable state). reasoning_effort high, not max: the productive-session data
## says the working SHAPE, not the model tier, is the lever (framing >
## capability). interaction: human — the agent may pause for the operator on a
## genuine mid-flight decision (kept rare by the contract).
agent campaign:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  input: campaign_input
  output: campaign_output
  system: campaign_system
  user: campaign_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: human

## verify_build — adaptive, stack-agnostic gate authoring (writes
## <scratch_dir>/verify.sh). fresh: whole-tree buildability, not a per-fix
## session. high, not max: mechanical capture of the repo's build/test.
agent verify_build:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  output: verify_plan
  system: verify_system
  user: verify_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: human

## verify_run — the DETERMINISTIC half: re-runs the agent-prepared
## <scratch_dir>/verify.sh and reports the REAL exit code. No language /
## package manager appears here — it executes whatever verify_build wrote, so
## the gate stays universal while the verdict stays deterministic. python3 for
## safe JSON + subprocess capture; double-quote-free body, no backticks (E012).
tool verify_run:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} SCRATCH={{vars.scratch_dir}} python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json
ws = os.environ.get('WS', '.')
scratch = os.environ.get('SCRATCH', '.')
try:
    os.makedirs(scratch, exist_ok=True)
except OSError:
    pass
script = os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.sh')
if not os.path.exists(script):
    print(json.dumps({'passed': True, 'skipped': True, 'exit_code': 0, 'log_tail': 'verify_build produced no verify.sh in the scratch dir; build/test gate SKIPPED (counted as pass, but surfaced) -- verify_build should write the script'}))
    raise SystemExit(0)
rc = 0
out = ''
try:
    p = subprocess.run(['sh', script], cwd=ws, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, timeout=1500)
    out = p.stdout or ''
    rc = p.returncode
except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as e:
    out = (e.output or '') + chr(10) + '[verify timed out after 1500s]'
    rc = 124
except Exception as e:
    out = 'verify_run could not execute the script: ' + str(e)
    rc = 1
try:
    with open(os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.log'), 'w', encoding='utf-8') as lf:
        lf.write(out)
except OSError:
    pass
print(json.dumps({'passed': rc == 0, 'skipped': False, 'exit_code': rc, 'log_tail': out[-4000:]}))
"`
  output: verify_result

## gate — the deterministic continuation decision. Reads the real gate result
## (outputs.verify_run.passed) AND the campaign's termination output
## (outputs.campaign.branch_clean), both fresh in the loop BODY (the same
## discipline whole_improve_loop's gate uses to read the loop-head safely).
## converged = green AND the branch diff is clean. On a RED pass fail_log
## carries the real build failure to the campaign; empty when green. No LLM,
## no shell.
compute gate:
  output: gate_state
  expr:
    converged: "outputs.verify_run.passed && outputs.campaign.branch_clean"
    fail_log: "if(outputs.verify_run.passed, '', outputs.verify_run.log_tail)"

## mr_gate lifts vars.open_mr into a bool output field so the two outgoing
## edges use the C012-exhaustive simple form. No LLM, no shell.
compute mr_gate:
  output: mr_gate_output
  expr:
    open_mr: "vars.open_mr"

## finalize_mr — opt-in MR/PR creation shipping the SERIES of per-pass commits.
## claude_code so the bundled forge-mr-create skill is mirrored and the board
## comment tool (native back-link) is available.
agent finalize_mr:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_BRANCH_IMPROVE_MR_MODEL:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_BRANCH_IMPROVE_MR_EFFORT:-medium}"
  input: finalize_mr_input
  output: finalize_mr_output
  system: finalize_mr_system
  user: finalize_mr_user
  tools: [bash, read_file, glob, grep]
  capabilities: [board.read, board.comment]
  tool_max_steps: 40

## ─── Workflow ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

workflow branch_improve_loop:
  ## Entry is the campaign itself — the mission starts working IMMEDIATELY
  ## (no blocking upfront diff-chunk scan). A fresh dispatch reviews the
  ## branch diff from a clean tree; a re-dispatch's campaign reads `git log`,
  ## re-diffs the branch, and continues from the commits earlier passes banked.
  entry: campaign

  ## Run in an isolated git worktree at .iterion/worktrees/<run-id>/. The bot
  ## mutates code AND commits; the worktree + finalize FF guarantees: (a) the
  ## operator's main checkout is untouched during the run, (b) on convergence
  ## the commits land on a persistent branch `iterion/run/<friendly-name>` AND
  ## fast-forward the operator's currently-checked-out branch (best-effort,
  ## skipped with a warning on conflict).
  worktree: auto

  ## Container isolation. campaign runs the repo's build, tests, linters and
  ## git; verify runs the build/test. Inline sandbox config (vs `mode: auto`)
  ## so the post_create that installs claude inside the container is honoured
  ## — see feature_dev/main.bot for the full rationale.
  sandbox:
    image: "ghcr.io/socialgouv/iterion-sandbox-full:edge"
    user: "1000:1000"
    mounts: ["type=bind,source=${localEnv:HOME}/.claude,target=/home/devbox/.claude,consistency=cached"]
    env:
      CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR: "/home/devbox/.claude"
    post_create: "set -e; claude --version >/dev/null 2>&1 || sudo npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code@latest; claude --version >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo 'claude install verification failed' >&2; exit 1; }"

  budget:
    max_parallel_branches: 1
    max_duration: "2h"
    max_cost_usd: 60

  compaction:
    threshold: 0.9
    preserve_recent: 8

  ## Each pass: the campaign reviews+improves the branch diff (committing each
  ## fix in stride), then the deterministic build/test gate re-checks the tree.
  campaign -> verify_build
  verify_build -> verify_run
  verify_run -> gate

  ## Converged (green AND the campaign reports the branch diff clean) → ship
  ## what is banked (optional MR, else finish).
  gate -> mr_gate when converged
  ## Not converged → another campaign pass. RED carries the failure log so the
  ## agent fixes what it broke; green-but-more-work carries an empty log so the
  ## agent simply keeps reviewing the diff. This closes the single declared
  ## loop (campaign→verify_build→verify_run→gate→campaign); the loop-back reads
  ## outputs.gate.fail_log (a BODY node — never the loop-head campaign's own
  ## output on its back-edge, which would read a loop-entry-frozen value).
  gate -> campaign as continuation_loop("{{vars.max_passes}}") with {
    fail_log: "{{outputs.gate.fail_log}}"
  }
  ## continuation_loop exhausted (max_passes hit while issues still remain) →
  ## ship what is banked anyway (every committed fix is real, landed work).
  ## Unconditional fallthrough after the loop-back retries are spent.
  gate -> mr_gate

  ## Converged (or capped) → optional MR path, else finish. finalize_mr pushes
  ## the run's HEAD (the series of per-pass commits) + opens the MR/PR and
  ## (when source_issue_ref is set) posts the URL back.
  mr_gate -> finalize_mr when open_mr with {
    workspace_dir:    "{{vars.workspace_dir}}",
    mr_branch:        "{{vars.mr_branch}}",
    mr_base:          "{{vars.mr_base}}",
    source_issue_ref: "{{vars.source_issue_ref}}",
    scope_notes:      "{{vars.scope_notes}}"
  }
  mr_gate -> done when not open_mr
  finalize_mr -> done
